602 / 'This is the mother of the world, the imperishable daughter of Himavat, / known in the universe as Umā, endowed with the power of austerity and yoga,
whose son was Brahmā, and who gave the office of Prajāpati; / and who, by the boon of his son, became the son of Brahmā;
who grants auspicious boons to us and to the deities, / the renowned one worshipped by all worlds and gods;
who abides having pervaded the entire aggregate of beings without remainder, / by whose self this whole imperishable universe is sustained;
who by his own will can collapse all this in half a wink: / this lady is the wife of that divine, wise Supreme Lord. / For she is capable in an instant, O lord of the sons of Diti,
either to burn the entire universe, or to carry it away, or to cast it into hell. / How did you, deluded daitya, not bow down to her?'

And the cows endowed with fortune, an auspicious rutting elephant,
a bull with a terrifying roar, maidens, hollow bamboo-reeds,
the set of eight auspicious items, and Dhanvantari the physician;
Śrī, Lakṣmī, and those that follow, and Vāruṇī intoxicating with intoxication;
and divine medicinal herbs, both cultivated and wild, together with humans,
all the trees and the like, and then Soma, king of the earth;
and drawing up the nectar, as well as that inaccessible poison,
and sacrifices and the like—all whatever had been collected there.

